ll the senescent cells  selectively  and so that's definitely interesting  work to keep an eye on  so how is analytics different to  cenomorphics  well as an alternative to killing  senescent cells  xenomorphics are proposed to act by  limiting the detrimental impacts of  senescent cells  largely through modulating the  senescence-associated secretory  phenotype  so in a way trying to reduce the amount  of inflammatory factors that are being  secreted  and so the way to do this is to inhibit  the proteins in a cell that  drive the expression of these different  susp components  i'd say one of the key proteins involved  in this process  is the mammalian target of rapamycin  mtor  and so unsurprisingly rapamycin which  inhibits centaur  and has also been shown to extend  lifespan in both male and female mice  it's thought to achieve that in part by  reducing the inflammatory phenotype of  senescent cells  and so again we have a nice figure  demonstrating how vapormycin  is inhibiting mtor another agent that's  worth mentioning  is metformin which is thought to as well  have some semimorphic potential  by inhibiting the action of nf kappa b  which is another key protein  that is thought to mediate the  upregulation of different genes that  make up the senescence-associated  secretory phenotype  and so the reason that i mentioned both  rapamycin and metformin is that they've  already been approved for  immunosuppression  and for type 2 diabetes respectively and  so this table that i'm showing now  shows other sonomorphics and their  action  as well as their current clinical trial  status that's have in most cases  either been approved for different  diseases or are being tested  in pre-clinical models so ignoring the  fact that these xenomorphic compounds  may have a multitude  of effects on cells and within mice or  within our bodies given the definition  of cenomorphics to inhibit  the pro-inflammatory sas factors it  could be considered  at the moment that synalytics may be  more beneficial than semimorphics  and there are three key reasons to  support this one  the useless analytics could be used as  an intermittent strategy  or as dr james kirkland director on  aging at the mayo clinic  likes to refer to as the hit and run  strategy  and so basically it means that you could  use synolytics  every so often as opposed to the  potential continuous administration of  sonomorphic agents  where repeated treatments would probably  be necessary  the second advantage is that removing  the senescent cells  eliminates the possibility of senescence  bypass  so that refers to a senescent cell that  can then escape senescence  and often that's due to the accumulation  of mutations  and so if it can escape senescence by  having more mutations  it's likely to then cause tumor genesis  so that's not good  and then the last benefit is the fact  that so far there hasn't  really been a direct demonstration that  the sasp is actually driving  aging related defects basically just  saying how  it's currently quite challenging to  separate the effect of inhibiting the  sasp from synalytics  when they use different transgenic mouse  models  but it is also worth mentioning that  it's not known whether sonolytics could  have detrimental consequences  especially when used in advanced stage  when the organism or  or humans in that case have a higher  abundance of senescent cells  moreover it's not known if there are any  detrimental consequences to long-term  use of cell analytics  but that's also why we have clinical  trials in the works  i said the last strategy is to activate  the immune system to remove senescent  cells  and so basically to exploit the fact  that organisms have an  intrinsic senolitic system that we refer  to as immunosurveillance  moreover it's possible that one of the  reasons why senescent cells accumulate  in aged tissues is due to a decline in  this immunosurveillance  and so boosting the natural immune  mechanisms of senescent 